"Internal Error ()\nciteturn13view0 [wordlim: 200] Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://mdpi-res.com/d_attachment/energies/energies-11-01033/article_deploy/energies-11-01033.pdf\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 1\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nEstimate Battery Model Parameters from HPPC Data - MATLAB & Simulink (https://www.mathworks.com/help/simscape-battery/ug/estimate-battery-model-parameters-from-hppc-data.html)\nciteturn13view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"turn12view2\",\"pattern\":\"SOC and temperature\"}); Total lines: 651\nL20: This example shows how to estimate the model parameters for a battery equivalent circuit model (ECM) from hybrid pulse power characterization (HPPC) data using Simscape™ Battery™. HPPC is a valuable technique in the battery industry and is one of the most common battery tests performed both at single-cell level or at system level. This technique involves applying a series of constant current pulses to a battery and measuring its terminal voltage and temperature responses at different operating conditions.\nL33: To create an `HPPCTest` object, use the `hppcTest` function. By default, the `HPPCTest` object assumes that the time, voltage, and current columns are named `\"Time\"`, `\"Voltage\"`, and `\"Current\"`, respectively. If these variables have different column name identifiers, you can specify them by using the optional name-value arguments.\nL34: \nL35:     hppcExp25degC = hppcTest(hppcData,...\nL36:         TimeVariable=\"time (s)\", ...\nL37:         VoltageVariable=\"voltage (V)\", ...\nL38:         CurrentVariable=\"current (A)\", ...\nL41: The `HPPCTest` object automatically calculates the battery SOC at every time step across the experiment, according to the input current measurement. The object uses these calculated SOC values to define the SOC breakpoints for every constant current pulse. The `TestSummary` property contains a summary of these SOC breakpoints as well as all other temperature and current breakpoints identified from the data.\nL42: Optionally, to improve the accuracy of the calculation of the SOC, the `hppcTest` function allows you to specify the battery capacity, the test initial SOC, and these column vectors:\nL43:   * `Charge` — If you specify this column variable and the `StateOfCharge` variable is empty, the `HPPCTest` object uses the values in this column to calculate the SOC breakpoints.\nL44: \nL45:   * `StateOfCharge` — if you specify this column variable, the `HPPCTest` object uses the values in this column to calculate the SOC breakpoints.\nL46: \nL47: To visualize a summary of the test and the identified charge and discharge pulses, use the cite6†`plot` function.\nL48: \nL49:     plot(hppcExp25degC)\nL50: cite7†Image: Figure contains an object of type simscape.battery.parameters.ui.hppcchart. L51: \nL52: The function automatically identifies the charge and discharge constant current pulses from the data according to these properties:\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nEvaluation of measurement data - Supplement 1 to the GUM (https://www.bipm.org/documents/20126/2071204/JCGM_101_2008_E.pdf)\nciteturn13view2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: application/pdf; Number of pages: 90; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"turn12view1\",\"pattern\":\"independent\"}); Total lines: 4253\nL259@P8: For a prescribed coverage probability, this Supplement can be used to provide any required coverage interval, including\nL260@P8: the probabilistically symmetric coverage interval and the shortest coverage interval.\nL261@P8: This Supplement applies to input quantities that are independent, where each such quantity is assigned an appropri\u0002ate PDF, or not independent, i.e. when some or all of these quantities are assigned a joint PDF.\nL505@P13: > is a vector variable describ\u0002ing the possible values of the vector quantity X. This X is considered as a random vector variable with (vector)\nL506@P13: expectation E(X) and covariance matrix V (X).\nL507@P13: 4.5 A PDF for more than one input quantity is often called joint even if all the input quantities are independent.\nL508@P13: 4.6 When the elements Xi of X are independent, the PDF for Xiis denoted by gXi(ξi).\nL509@P13: 4.7 The PDF for Y is denoted by gY (η) and the distribution function for Y by GY (η).\nL679@P17: gX2\nL680@P17: (ξ2)\nL681@P17: ✲\nL682@P17: gX1\nL683@P17: (ξ1)\nL684@P17: Y = f(X) ✲\nL685@P17: gY (η)\nL686@P17: Figure 2 — Illustration of the propagation of distributions for N = 3 independent input quantities (5.4.4)\nL687@P17: 5.4.5 In practice, only for simple cases can the propagation of distributions be implemented without making\nL688@P17: approximations. The GUM uncertainty framework implements one approximate method, and MCM another. For a\nL689@P17: small but important subset of problems, the GUM uncertainty framework is exact. MCM is never exact, but is more\nL738@P19: for a model having N = 3 independent input quantities X = (X1, X2, X3)\nL739@P19: >, which are estimated by xi with asso\u0002ciated standard uncertainties u(xi), i = 1, 2, 3. The output quantity Y is estimated by y, with associated standard\nL740@P19: uncertainty u(y).\nL741@P19: a) Obtain from the PDFs for the input quantities X = (X1, . . . , XN )\nL742@P19: > the expectations x = (x1, . . . , xN )> and the\nL743@P19: standard deviations (standard uncertainties) u(x) = [u(x1), . . . , u(xN )]>. Use instead the joint PDF for X if\nL744@P19: pairs of the Xi are not independent (in which case they have non-zero covariance).\nL745@P19: b) Set the degrees of freedom (infinite or finite) associated with each u(xi).\nL746@P19: c) For each pair i, j for which Xi and Xj are not independent, obtain from the joint PDF for Xi and Xj the\nL747@P19: covariance (mutual uncertainty) u(xi, xj ) associated with xi and xj .\nL748@P19: d) Form the partial derivatives of first order of f(X) with respect to X.\nL749@P19: e) Calculate y, the model evaluated at X equal to x.\nL750@P19: f) Calculate the model sensitivity coefficients [GUM:1995 5.1.3] as the above partial derivatives evaluated at x.\nL751@P19: g) Calculate the standard uncertainty u(y) by combining u(x), the u(xi, xj ), and the model sensitivity coeffi\u0002cients [GUM:1995 formulæ (10), (13)].\nL752@P19: h) Calculate νeff, the effective degrees of freedom associated with u(y), using the Welch-Satterthwaite for\u0002mula [GUM:1995 formula (G.2b)].\nL753@P19: i) Calculate the expanded uncertainty Up, and hence a coverage interval (for a stipulated coverage probability p)\nL754@P19: for Y , regarded as a random variable, by forming the appropriate multiple of u(y) through taking the probability\nL755@P19: distribution of (Y − y)/u(y) as a standard Gaussian distribution (νeff = ∞) or t-distribution (νeff < ∞).\nL756@P19: x1, u(x1) ✲\nL757@P19: x2, u(x2) ✲\nL758@P19: x3, u(x3) ✲\nL759@P19: Y = f(X) ✲ y, u(y)\nL760@P19: Figure 3 — Illustration of the law of propagation of uncertainty for N = 3 independent input quantities\nL761@P19: (5.4.4, 5.6.3)\nL762@P19: 5.7 Conditions for valid application of the GUM uncertainty framework for linear models\nL763@P19: 5.7.1 No condition is necessary for the valid application of the law of propagation of uncertainty to linear models\nL764@P19: (models that are linear in the Xi).\nL765@P19: 5.7.2 A coverage interval can be determined, in terms of the information provided in the GUM, under the following\nL766@P19: conditions:\nL767@P19: a) the Welch-Satterthwaite formula is adequate for calculating the effective degrees of freedom associated\nL768@P19: with u(y) [GUM:1995 G.4.1], when one or more of the u(xi) has an associated degrees of freedom that is fi\u0002nite;\nL769@P19: b) the Xi are independent when the degrees of freedom associated with the u(xi) are finite;\nL770@P19: c) the PDF for Y can adequately be approximated by a Gaussian distribution or a scaled and shifted t-distribution.\nL771@P19: 12\nL772@P19-20: c JCGM 2008— All rights reservedJCGM 101:2008\nL773@P20: NOTE 1 Condition a) is required in order that Y can be characterized by an appropriate scaled and shifted t-distribution.\nL774@P20: NOTE 2 Condition b) is required because the GUM does not treat Xi that are not independent in conjunction with finite\nL775@P20: degrees of freedom.\nL776@P20: NOTE 3 Condition c) is satisfied when each Xi is assigned a Gaussian distribution. It is also satisfied when the conditions\nL777@P20: for the central limit theorem [GUM:1995 G.2] hold.\nL778@P20: NOTE 4 The GUM uncertainty framework may not validly be applicable when there is an Xi whose assigned distribution is\nL779@P20: non-Gaussian and the corresponding contribution to u(y) is dominant.\nL780@P20: 5.7.3 When the conditions in 5.7.2 hold, the results from the application of the GUM uncertainty framework can\nL781@P20: be expected to be valid for linear models. These conditions apply in many circumstances.\nL782@P20: 5.8 Conditions for valid application of the GUM uncertainty framework for non-linear models\nL783@P20: 5.8.1 The law of propagation of uncertainty can validly be applied for non-linear models under the following\nL784@P20: conditions:\nL785@P20: a) f is continuously differentiable with respect to the elements Xi of X in the neighbourhood of the best estimates xi\nL786@P20: of the Xi;\nL787@P20: b) condition a) applies for all derivatives up to the appropriate order;\nL788@P20: c) the Xiinvolved in significant higher-order terms of a Taylor series approximation to f(X) are independent;\nL789@P20: d) the PDFs assigned to Xiinvolved in higher-order terms of a Taylor series approximation to f(X) are Gaussian;\nL790@P20: e) higher-order terms that are not included in the Taylor series approximation to f(X) are negligible.\nL791@P20: NOTE 1 Condition a) is necessary for the applicability of the law of propagation of uncertainty based on a first-order Taylor\nL792@P20: series approximation to f(X) when the non-linearity of f is insignificant [GUM:1995 5.1.2].\nL793@P20: NOTE 2 Condition b) is necessary for the application of the law of propagation of uncertainty based on a higher-order Taylor\nL794@P20: series approximation to f(X) [GUM:1995 5.1.2]. An expression for the most important terms of next highest order to be\nL795@P20: included are given in the GUM [GUM:1995 5.1.2 note].\nL796@P20: NOTE 3 Condition c) relates to the statement in the GUM [GUM:1995 5.1.2 note] concerning significant model non-linearity\nL797@P20: in the case of independent Xi. The GUM does not consider Xi that are not independent in this context.\nL798@P20: NOTE 4 Condition d) constitutes a correction to the statement in the GUM [GUM:1995 5.1.2 note] that the version of the\nL799@P20: law of propagation of uncertainty using higher-order terms is based on the symmetry of the PDFs for the Xi [19, 27].\nL800@P20: NOTE 5 If the analytical determination of the higher derivatives, required when the non-linearity of the model is significant,\nL801@P20: is difficult or error-prone, suitable software for automatic differentiation can be used. Alternatively, these derivatives can be\nL802@P20: approximated numerically using finite differences [5]. (The GUM provides a finite-difference formula for partial derivatives of\nL803@P20: first order [GUM:1995 5.1.3 note 2].) Care should be taken, however, because of the effects of subtractive cancellation when\nL804@P20: forming differences between numerically close model values.\nL805@P20: 5.8.2 A coverage interval can be determined, in terms of the information provided in the GUM, when conditions a),\nL981@P24: 6.1.2 Generally, a joint PDF gX(ξ) is assigned to the input quantities X = (X1, . . . , XN )\nL982@P24: >. See 6.4.8.4 note 2.\nL983@P24: \nL984@P24-25: c JCGM 2008— All rights reserved 17JCGM 101:2008\nL985@P25: 6.1.3 When the Xi are independent, PDFs gXi(ξi) are assigned individually based on an analysis of a series of\nL986@P25: indications (Type A evaluation of uncertainty) or based on scientific judgement using information [50] such as historical\nL987@P25: data, calibrations, and expert judgement (Type B evaluation of uncertainty) [GUM:1995 3.3.5].\nL988@P25: 6.1.4 When some of the Xi are mutually independent, PDFs are assigned individually to them and a joint PDF\nL989@P25: to the remainder.\nL990@P25: NOTE It may be possible to remove some or all dependencies by re-expressing relevant input quantities in terms of more\nL991@P25: fundamental independent input quantities on which the original input quantities depend [GUM:1995 F1.2.4, H.1.2]. Such\nL992@P25: changes can simplify both the application of the law of propagation of uncertainty and the propagation of distributions. Details\nL993@P25: and examples are available [15].\nL994@P25: 6.1.5 Information relevant to the assignment of PDFs to the Xiis contained in the GUM [GUM:1995 4.3].\nL995@P25: 6.1.6 Comprehensive guidance on the assignment of PDFs individually or jointly to the Xiis beyond the scope\nL996@P25: of this Supplement. Such assigned PDFs encode the knowledge and expertise of the metrologist who formulates the\nL997@P25: model and who is ultimately responsible for the quality of the final results.\nL998@P25: 6.1.7 A standard text on probability distributions is Evans, Hastings and Peacock [18].\nL999@P25: 6.2 Bayes’ theorem\nL1000@P25: 6.2.1 Suppose that information about an input quantity X consists of a series of indications regarded as realiza\u0002tions of independent, identically distributed random variables characterized by a specified PDF, but with unknown\nL1001@P25: expectation and variance. Bayes’ theorem can be used to calculate a PDF for X, where X is taken to be equal to the\nL2059@P42: quantity can be markedly asymmetric, and thus the GUM uncertainty framework can yield invalid results, even if all\nL2060@P42: higher-order terms are taken into account. Instances where the input quantities are independent and not independent\nL2061@P42: are treated.\nL2062@P42: 9.1.8 The fourth example (see 9.5) is that in the GUM concerned with gauge block calibration [GUM:1995 H.1].\nL2063@P42: The information given there concerning the model input quantities is interpreted, PDFs accordingly assigned to these\nL2071@P42: the input quantities Xi, regarded as independent. The Xi and hence the output quantity Y have dimension 1. For the\nL2072@P42: first set, each gXi(ξi) is a standard Gaussian PDF (with Xi having expectation zero and standard deviation unity).\nL2073@P42: For the second set, each gXi(ξi) is a rectangular PDF, also with Xi having expectation zero and standard deviation\nL2074@P42: unity. The third set is identical to the second except that the PDF for gX4\nL2075@P42: (ξ4) has a standard deviation of ten.\n"
